[h1]ServerGuide Images Example[/h1]

[b]A template mod for shipping the images used in your [url=https://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=3743488819]Server Guide[/url] pages. No Lua — just a packed media/ folder you copy and fill with your own PNGs.[/b]

[i]Requires the [b][url=https://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=3743488819]Server Guide[/url][/b] mod.[/i]

[hr][/hr]
[h2]Why images MUST live in a mod[/h2]
Server Guide streams your guide [b]text[/b] live from the server, so you can edit rules and guides on the fly. [b]Images do not work that way[/b] — and this is a hard limit of the game, not the mod:
[list]
[*]Project Zomboid only shows images that were scanned into its [b]texture map at startup[/b], and it scans the [b][i]media/[/i] folders of installed mods[/b] — nothing else. There is no "live" image loading.
[*]The [b]<IMAGE>[/b] tag is resolved on the machine that displays it (the client), by texture [b]name[/b], from that startup map.
[*]A PNG dropped into the server's live text folder ([i]~/Zomboid/Lua/ServerGuide/[/i]) is never in that map — and in multiplayer its bytes never travel to the client anyway.
[/list]
[b]Conclusion:[/b] every image you want in a guide has to be packaged inside a mod's [i]media/[/i] folder, and — for multiplayer — that mod has to be [b]published on the Workshop[/b] so each client downloads it. This template is that mod: copy it, add your PNGs, publish it.

[hr][/hr]
[h2]How to use[/h2]
[olist]
[*][b]Copy this mod[/b] and rename it ([i]name[/i] and [i]id[/i] in [i]mod.info[/i]) to your own.
[*][b]Drop your PNGs[/b] in [i]media/ui/guias/[/i] (or your own unique subfolder).
[*][b]Reference them[/b] in your Server Guide pages by the path starting at [i]media/[/i]:
[/olist]
[code]<IMAGECENTRE:media/ui/guias/map.png,512,512>
<IMAGE:media/ui/guias/logo.png>[/code]
In singleplayer / a local host, that's all — a local mod is enough. For a dedicated / multiplayer server, publish your copy to the Workshop (next).

[hr][/hr]
[h2]Publish your image mod to the Workshop[/h2]
Clients only get your images if the mod is on the Workshop and listed on the server. Do this once per image mod (and again to update it):
[olist]
[*][b]Lay out the Workshop folder.[/b] Put your renamed copy inside a Workshop item folder:
[code]~/Zomboid/Workshop/YourItemName/
  workshop.txt
  preview.png
  Contents/
    mods/
      YourModId/        <- your copy: mod.info + 42/media/...[/code]
[*][b]Write [i]workshop.txt[/i][/b] (in [i]YourItemName/[/i]) with the listing metadata, e.g.:
[code]version=1
id=
title=My Server Images
description=Images for my server's guides.
tags=Build 42;Multiplayer
visibility=public[/code]
Leave [i]id=[/i] empty for a brand-new item — Steam fills it on first upload. Add a [i]preview.png[/i] next to it (the thumbnail).
[*][b]Upload from in-game.[/b] Launch Project Zomboid → main menu → [b]Workshop[/b] → [b]Create and Update Item[/b] → pick [i]YourItemName[/i] → review title/description/preview → [b]Submit[/b].
[*][b]Make it Public.[/b] Open the new item's Workshop page and set visibility to [b]Public[/b] — dedicated servers download anonymously and [b]cannot[/b] fetch Hidden / Friends-only items (the download fails and can crash the server).
[*][b]List it on the server.[/b] Copy the item's [b]ID[/b] (the number in its Workshop URL) into [b]WorkshopItems=[/b], and your mod's [i]id[/i] into [b]Mods=[/b], in the server config. Restart the server.
[/olist]
[b]Updating later:[/b] change your files, run [b]Create and Update Item[/b] on the same item again (keep the [i]id=[/i] that Steam wrote into workshop.txt).

[hr][/hr]
[h2]Path rules (important)[/h2]
[list]
[*][b]The mod name is NOT in the path.[/b] A PNG at [i]<mod>/42/media/ui/guias/map.png[/i] is registered as [i]media/ui/guias/map.png[/i]. Use that.
[*][b]Flat namespace.[/b] Texture names are shared across all mods — files with the same path collide. Use a unique subfolder, e.g. [i]media/ui/myserver/map.png[/i].
[*][b]No absolute paths.[/b] A path like [i]/home/.../Zomboid/Lua/...[/i] resolves on the client, which doesn't have the file. Always use the [i]media/[/i]-relative name.
[/list]

[hr][/hr]
[h2]Image size[/h2]
There is no hard pixel limit (it's bounded by the GPU's max texture size, normally well above 4096px), but for performance:
[list]
[*]Use [b]power-of-two[/b] dimensions (256, 512, 1024…). The game pads other sizes up to the next power of two in video memory — a 513px image costs as much as 1024px.
[*]The image is scaled to the [b]width,height you pass in <IMAGECENTRE:…,w,h>[/b], so don't ship a file much bigger than you display.
[*]Keep dimensions at/under ~2048px to be safe on older GPUs, and keep the file small — every client downloads this mod.
[/list]

[hr][/hr]
[h2]Multiplayer[/h2]
Reminder: local mods (in the server's [i]~/Zomboid/mods[/i]) are [b]not[/b] sent to clients — for images to reach players in MP your copy must be on the Workshop and listed in [b]WorkshopItems=[/b] (see "Publish your image mod to the Workshop" above). Singleplayer / local host needs no publishing.

[hr][/hr]
[h2]Languages[/h2]
No in-game text — it only ships images. Documentation in English and Português (Brasil).

[hr][/hr]
[i]Build 42.[/i]

[hr][/hr]
[h2]Use it freely[/h2]

This is a [b]template[/b], meant to be copied. Duplicate it, rename it, drop in your
own images and publish it for your server — no permission needed. Do whatever you
want with it.
